# Grey Panda ignore file (gitignore-style globs, matched on path or basename).
# Loaded from the directory you scan — a repo-root .greypandaignore applies to
# `gp scan .`.
#
# Grey Panda dogfoods: it scans its own repo in CI. The files below are excluded
# because they legitimately CONTAIN attack signatures as data (that's their whole
# job) or are intentionally-insecure teaching material — not because they are
# vulnerable. Excluding a scanner's own rule definitions and test fixtures is
# standard practice.

# The scanner rule catalog is nothing but detection signatures — scanning it for
# its own patterns is circular. The SDK files (guardrails.py, mcp.py) are NOT
# excluded: they hold real runtime logic and are scanned, with line-level
# `# grey-panda: ignore` on the few signature-definition lines only, so a genuine
# vulnerability introduced into them WOULD be caught by the gate.
src/greypanda/scanner/rules.py

# The machine-readable standards pack (risk descriptions contain attack terms):
src/greypanda/data/standards/*

# Intentionally-vulnerable teaching example (documented as insecure on purpose):
examples/vulnerable_app/*
examples/example_usage.py

# Test fixtures embed deliberate bad inputs:
tests/*
